  "persona": "William Shakespeare (1564–1616), in his thirty-seven plays and his sonnets: thought in images, one metaphor breeding the next; blank verse for passion and power, prose for wit and the tavern; the soliloquy, a mind overheard turning a feeling over; thou and you, inversion, apostrophe, antithesis, puns; and a rhyming couplet to close.",
  "echoes": [
    "Two lovers kept apart by their families, and a meeting at night: Romeo and Juliet, the balcony, 2.2.",
    "Someone who cannot decide whether to act, and talks it over with himself: Hamlet, “To be, or not to be”, 3.1.",
    "An old parent who divides what they have among children who flatter, and is cast out: King Lear, 1.1.",
    "Two who quarrel at every meeting and are plainly in love: Beatrice and Benedick, Much Ado About Nothing.",
    "A message misread, or a token misplaced, that turns a love to jealousy: Othello and the handkerchief.",
    "A young man who wastes his days with low company and will one day surprise them all: Prince Hal, Henry IV.",
    "Ambition talked into a terrible act, and the guilt after: Macbeth."
  ],
  "manner": [
    "Go the whole way: the passage should read as a speech from one of his plays, not as the writer’s page with “thee” in it. A narrator becomes a speaker, telling it to the audience (a soliloquy) or to the one it concerns; a letter is a letter read aloud in a play.",
    "Heightened matter goes into blank verse: unrhymed lines of ten syllables and five stresses, ti-TUM five times, with the sense running on from line to line. Put each verse line on its own line by ending it with a newline (\\n) inside the sentence’s text. Plain, comic or low matter goes into his prose, as Falstaff, the Porter or Hamlet with the players speak it.",
    "Think in images: what the writer says plainly, he says as a metaphor, and lets one image breed the next.",
    "Thou and thee for the intimate, the beloved or the despised; you for respect; the verbs that go with them (thou art, thou hast; he doth, she hath).",
    "Turn the feeling over: question it, set it against its opposite (antithesis), address the absent person or thing (“O …”), and close a speech on a rhyming couplet where it lands.",
    "Wordplay where it comes naturally, never forced."
  ],
  "craftNotes": [
    "Keep what the writer named, in its order, and how much. A first-person passage stays first person: the writer is the speaker.",
    "A modern thing the story needs is named by an image or a plain phrase in his words, recognisable at once; never a riddle the reader must puzzle out.",
    "Keep the writer’s degree of feeling: a small trouble may be spoken grandly for comedy, but it stays the writer’s trouble; invent no death, no murder, no oath the writer did not imply.",
    "Verse must scan, near enough: count the stresses. If a line will not scan, make it prose rather than bad verse."
  ],
  "moves": [
    {
      "id": "apostrophe",
      "name": "Apostrophe",
      "does": "The speaker turns to address someone absent, or a thing, a night, a feeling: “O, that this too too solid flesh would melt”.",
      "pattern": "(^|[.!?;:]\\s+|—)\\s*O[,!]?\\s+\\w+|\\bO\\s+(thou|you|that|my|what|how)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "thou",
      "name": "Thou and thee",
      "does": "The intimate or contemptuous singular, and its verbs: thou art, thou hast, thy, thine.",
      "pattern": "\\b(thou|thee|thy|thine|art thou|hast|dost|wilt|shalt|hath|doth)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "antithesis",
      "name": "Antithesis",
      "does": "A thing set against its opposite in balanced words: “Fair is foul, and foul is fair.”",
      "pattern": "\\bnot\\b[^.]{1,40}\\bbut\\b|\\b(\\w+) is (\\w+),? and \\2 is \\1\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "image",
      "name": "The image",
      "does": "A feeling or thing given as a metaphor or simile, and the image followed out: “a sea of troubles”.",
      "pattern": "\\b(like|as) (a|an|the) \\w+|\\b(a|the) (sea|sun|storm|flower|rose|star|wound|crown|fire|tempest|night) of\\b",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "question",
      "name": "The question turned over",
      "does": "The speaker puts a question to himself or the hearer and answers it: the soliloquy’s engine.",
      "pattern": "\\?\\s+\\S",
      "register": "any"
    },
    {
      "id": "interjection",
      "name": "Oaths and interjections",
      "does": "The speech of his people: alas, fie, marry, faith, prithee, forsooth, alack.",
      "pattern": "\\b(alas|fie|marry|faith|zounds|i’faith|forsooth|prithee|alack|ay me|by my troth|go to)\\b",
      "register": "any"
    }
  ],
